{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF4", "text": "Morgan, from clinical information technology I can report that a high-priority electronic medication-record alert is ready to be deployed at 16:00 today. This alert would fire at the point of order entry, warning clinicians about concentration selection and requiring acknowledgment before the order can be released. This directly addresses the selection errors that Leah identified, adding a system-level safeguard at the ordering stage even if both concentrations remain stocked in the automated dispensing cabinets."}
```

```json
{"type": "say", "text": "Thanks, Morgan. To answer your question about pharmacy supporting the volume of prepared syringes — yes, pharmacy can support supplying only pharmacy-prepared 1 mg/mL syringes during a temporary hold. We have the staffing and compounding capacity to handle the inpatient volume, and this approach would eliminate the selection risk at the cabinet level entirely. One additional consideration: by standardizing to a single concentration prepared by pharmacy, we also reduce the cognitive load on nursing at the point of administration, which may help with the competency gap Tessa identified. I don't have any further concerns beyond what I've already raised — the three selection errors I verified since Monday are the core issue, and removing both concentrations from ADC stock addresses that directly."}
```

